## Typical Arguments for plot functions

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{add=TRUE}: Add to an existing plot?
\item \code{axes=FALSE}: Plot axes?
\item \code{log="x"}, \code{log="y"} or \code{log="xy"}: Log. axes?
\item \code{type="l"}: Plot lines instead of points 
\item \code{xlab, ylab}: Axis labels
\item \code{main}: Figure title
\item \code{sub}: Sub-title
\end{itemize}

# Low-level Graphics Functions
## Some low-level functions

These functions can be used to add elements to a plot.
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{points(x, y)}
\item \code{lines(x, y)}
\item \code{text(x, y, labels, ...)}
\item \code{abline(a, b)} or \code{abline(h=y)} or \code{abline(v=x)}
\item \code{polygon(x, y, ...)}
\item \code{legend(x, y, legend, ...)}
\item \code{title(main, sub)}
\item \code{axis(side, ...)}
\end{itemize}


## Graphical parameter list: par
\small

R maintains a list of graphics parameters to control
line style, colors, figure arrangement and text justification. 

A separate list of graphics parameters is maintained for each active device.

```{r}
oldpar <- par(col=4, pch=4)
plot(x, y)
par(oldpar)	# restore the original parameters
{r}

Many parameters from \code{par()} can also be passed to \code{plot()}.

Try \code{par()} and \code{?par}


## Important parameters in par

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{pch=4}: Plotting symbol (0-25) 
\item \code{lty=2}: Line type  
\item \code{lwd=2}: Line width 
\item \code{col=2}: Color for points, lines, etc. 
\item \code{cex=1.5}: Character expansion (e.g., 50\% larger than default text size) 
\item \code{mai=c(1, 0.5, 0.5, 0)}: Widths of the bottom, left, top and 
right margins, respectively, measured in inches.
\end{itemize}

## Saving a plot as an image

```{r}
png(file="plot.png")	# open device
plot(x, y)
dev.off()		# close device
```

\begin{block}{}
Other devices are \code{jpeg(), tiff(),} \code{pdf(), postscript(),} \code{win.metafile()} 
(Windows). 

Use \code{?Devices} for a complete list.
\end{block}

## Interactive and Advanced Graphics
Interactive Graphics are available via several extension packages:
\begin{itemize}
\item \pkg{ggplot2}: Grammar of graphics.  
\item \pkg{rggobi}: GGobi interactive graphics system.
\item \pkg{iplots}: Java based plotting (alpha blending, brushing, selection, etc.)
\item \pkg{playwith}: Build interactive versions of R graphics 
\item \pkg{rgl}: OpenGL
\end{itemize}

Advanced Graphics
\begin{itemize}
\item \pkg{ggplot2}: Grammar of graphics. Produces elegant visualizations 
(see \url{http://ggplot2.org/}).  
\item \pkg{grid}: Advanced graphics can be programmed using flexible
low level ploting functions (viewports, different coordinate systems
and units, lines, points, text, etc.) See also package \pkg{lattice}.
\end{itemize}



# Exercises
## Exercises

\begin{enumerate}
\item Plot a \code{sin(x)/x}. Hint: Trigonometric functions in R use angles in
radians (see \code{sin})
\item 
The ``cars'' data set gives the speed of cars and the distances taken to stop.
     Note that the data were recorded in the 1920s.
Plot the ``cars'' data set as a scatter plot. Plot all 
data points with distances taken to stop greater than 80 in red. 
\item Plot histograms for speed and dist in ``cars''.
\end{enumerate}

